using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;

public static List<XmlElement> GetElementsByName(string xml, string name)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(xml))
        throw new ArgumentException("XML document is empty", nameof(xml));

    var doc = new XmlDocument();
    try
    {
        doc.LoadXml(xml);
    }
    catch (XmlException ex)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("Invalid XML document", nameof(xml), ex);
    }

    var navigator = doc.CreateNavigator();
    var xpath = $"//*[local-name()='{name}']";
    var elements = new List<XmlElement>();

    foreach (var node in navigator.Select(xpath))
    {
        if (node is XmlElement element)
            elements.Add(element);
    }

    return elements;
}
```
This function takes an XML document as a string and a name to match, and returns a list of all elements that match the given name in the XML document. If no elements are found, it returns `null`.

The function uses the `XmlDocument` class to load the XML document into memory, and then creates an `XPathNavigator` object to navigate the document using XPath expressions. The XPath expression used is `"//*[local-name()='{name}']"` which selects all elements with a local name of `{name}`.

The function uses a `foreach` loop to iterate over the nodes selected by the XPath expression, and adds each element that matches the given name to a list of `XmlElement` objects. If no elements are found, the list is empty.

