function runModuleTests(moduleName, testCases) {
  const module = require(moduleName);
  let totalTests = 0;
  let executedTests = [];
  let errors = [];

  for (const testCase of testCases) {
    try {
      console.log(`Starting test ${testCase.label} for module ${moduleName}`);
      if (testCase.setup) {
        testCase.setup();
      }
      testCase.f();
      if (testCase.teardown) {
        testCase.teardown();
      }
      executedTests.push(testCase.label);
    } catch (error) {
      errors.push(`Error in test ${testCase.label}: ${error}`);
    } finally {
      totalTests++;
      blueslip.reset();
    }
  }

  return {
    totalTests,
    executedTests,
    errors,
  };
}
```
This code defines a function `runModuleTests` that takes three parameters: `moduleName`, an array of test case objects, and a mocking library called `blueslip`. The function dynamically loads the specified module using the `require` function, then iterates over each test case in the array. For each test case, it logs the start of the test, executes the `setup` function if provided, runs the test function `f`, executes the `teardown` function if provided, and resets the mocking library `blueslip`. If an error occurs during the execution of a test case, it is caught and added to the `errors` array. The function returns a summary object containing the total number of tests executed, the names of all executed tests, and any errors encountered during the test execution.']

