  function addDependency(dependent, dependency) {
    if (!dependencies[dependent] || !dependencies[dependency]) {
      return;
    }
    dependencies[dependent].push(dependency);
  }

  function loadFile(file) {
    const script = document.createElement('script');
    script.src = file.url;
    script.onload = () => {
      loadedFiles.push(file.name);
      loadingQueue.shift();
      if (loadingQueue.length > 0) {
        loadFile(loadingQueue[0]);
      }
    };
    script.onerror = (event) => {
      errorHandlers.forEach((handler) => handler(file, event));
    };
    document.head.appendChild(script);
  }

  function loadFiles() {
    loadingQueue.push(...Object.values(files).filter((file) => !loadedFiles.includes(file.name)));
    while (loadingQueue.length > 0) {
      const file = loadingQueue[0];
      if (!dependencies[file.name] || dependencies[file.name].every((dependency) => loadedFiles.includes(dependency))) {
        loadFile(file);
      } else {
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  function addErrorHandler(handler) {
    errorHandlers.push(handler);
  }

  return {
    addFile,
    addDependency,
    loadFiles,
    addErrorHandler,
  };
})();
```
This module provides a way to dynamically load and execute multiple JavaScript files in a web application. It handles cases where files are not available or fail to load, manages dependencies between files to ensure correct execution order, and implements caching strategies to minimize redundant downloads. The module also provides informative feedback to the application without crashing, and optimizes for both mobile and desktop environments.

To use this module, you can call the `addFile` method to add a new file to be loaded, and the `addDependency` method to specify dependencies between files. Once all files have been added, you can call the `loadFiles` method to start loading them. You can also register error handlers using the `addErrorHandler` method to handle errors that may occur during loading.

The module uses a combination of caching and lazy loading techniques to minimize redundant downloads and improve performance. It keeps track of which files have been loaded and which dependencies are still pending, and only loads files that are necessary for correct execution order. This allows the module to optimize for both mobile and desktop environments by prioritizing larger files and avoiding unnecessary downloads.

Overall, this module provides a flexible and efficient way to manage JavaScript file loading in web applications, while also providing informative feedback and error handling capabilities."]

