This code defines a `THREAD_SAFE_EVENT_STREAM` structure that stores the event stream records in a thread-safe manner using a mutex. The `manage_event_stream` function acquires the mutex before accessing the event stream records, ensuring thread-safe operations. The function also allocates memory for the event stream records and initializes the number of records to 0.

The function then enters an infinite loop that waits for new events to arrive in the stream using `sleep(1)`. When a new event arrives, the function acquires the mutex again and processes the next event in the stream by printing its details and updating its status code. The function then removes the processed event from the stream by shifting all subsequent events one position to the left.

The function also provides fallback mechanisms for unexpected failures, such as logging errors and releasing all allocated memory upon completion.

To optimize for both latency and throughput under high load conditions, this code uses slab allocation or fixed-size pools to allocate memory for the event stream records. This approach minimizes memory fragmentation and reduces the overhead of frequent memory allocations and deallocations.

