This code implements a message queue system that can efficiently manage a variety of event types, each characterized by a unique EVENT_ID, TIMESTAMP, EVENT_SOURCE, and EVENT_PAYLOAD. The system supports real-time processing and high throughput, with minimal latency and resource contention.

The message queue is implemented using a circular buffer data structure, where events are stored in an array of size `size`. The head and tail indices are used to keep track of the first and last elements in the queue, respectively. The mutex and condition variable are used for synchronization between threads, ensuring that only one thread can access the queue at a time.

The `enqueue` function takes an event ID, timestamp, event source, and event payload as input, and adds it to the end of the queue. If the queue is full, the function waits until there is space available in the queue before adding the new event. The `dequeue` function removes the first event from the queue and returns its EVENT_ID, timestamp, event source, and event payload as output.

The system also includes backpressure handling mechanisms to control the rate of event submission, preventing the queue from becoming overwhelmed. This is achieved by using a condition variable to signal when a new event is available in the queue, allowing other threads to wait until there is space available before adding new events.

In addition, the system supports real-time processing and high throughput by using non-blocking operations and timeout-based operations to handle lock contention. The `enqueue` function uses a semaphore to acquire the mutex lock, which allows other threads to wait until the lock is available before adding new events. The `dequeue` function also uses a semaphore to acquire the mutex lock, allowing other threads to wait until there are events available in the queue before removing them.

The system also includes deadlock avoidance mechanisms to prevent deadlocks when multiple threads access the queue simultaneously. This is achieved by using non-blocking operations and timeout-based operations to handle lock contention. The `enqueue` function uses a semaphore to acquire the mutex lock, which allows other threads to wait until the lock is available before adding new events. The `dequeue` function also uses a semaphore to acquire the mutex lock, allowing other threads to wait until there are events available in the queue before removing them.

The system also includes memory deallocation strategies for managing memory when events cannot be allocated due to constraints. This is achieved by prioritizing retaining critical event data over less important information, ensuring data integrity and performance.

Finally, the system supports graceful degradation in resource-constrained environments by reducing the frequency of event processing and logging activities, avoiding abrupt terminations. This is achieved by implementing strategies for managing memory when events cannot be allocated due to constraints. The system prioritizes retaining critical event data over less important information, ensuring data integrity and performance.

